#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Install Gentoo on an HP Googlebook (Quartz) from another Linux PC.

usage: tools/googlebook-installer [--from PHASE] [--only PHASE] [--ip ADDR]

Run it from a checkout of this repository, as a normal user (it asks for
sudo once, to assemble the root filesystem). It walks you through unlocking
the laptop and starting its fastboot mode, then:

  check     checks the tools this PC needs
  settings  asks for a hostname, user, passwords, time zone and locale
  device    connects to the laptop's fastboot over Ethernet
  stock     optionally reads vbmeta_a and the DSP firmware (modem_a) from
            HP's recovery USB drive or image
  download  fetches the Gentoo stage3, the kernel source and the firmware
            files, and checks them against known checksums
  kernel    builds Linux with the Googlebook patches
  images    builds the boot images (kernel, rescue initramfs, DTB, vbmeta)
  rootfs    unpacks the stage3, adds the kernel modules, firmware and
            configuration, and makes an ext4 image of it (needs sudo)
  flash     writes the boot images to Gentoo's slot (usually B) and the root
            filesystem to userdata, and boots it
  finish    waits for the new system to come up on the network

Progress is kept in work/state.json, so running it again resumes where it
stopped. --from PHASE redoes PHASE and everything after it; --only PHASE
runs just that phase.

Nothing is written to the laptop before the flash phase, which asks first.
Only Gentoo's slot (the one stock Android isn't on, usually B) and userdata
are written; the other slot keeps stock Android.
"""

